From 0cc8dee85aec140d2c4bf2529ef3f171fe263f88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vrisan <vrisanshah@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 05:38:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] phase-R-ai-cost-optimization

Milestone 4.1 (caching) + 4.3 (model tiering), plus the two specific
cost cuts from the Aug 4 emails (writing-competition AI feedback,
comment moderation) and the parent-digest opt-in redesign.

lib/gemini.ts:
- callGemini() now takes an optional tier ('lite' | 'flash' | 'pro'),
  defaulting to 'lite'. Nothing routes to 'pro' yet -- no call site
  in the app currently needs it, so there was nothing honest to wire
  up for Milestone 4.3's high-reasoning tier without inventing a use
  case. The routing is in place for when one shows up.
- callGemini() now takes an optional cacheKey + cacheTtlMinutes. When
  set, identical requests reuse a cached Supabase row instead of
  hitting the API. Cache reads/writes fail open -- a cache outage
  degrades to normal (uncached) behavior, never breaks a request.

supabase/migrations/027_ai_cost_optimization.sql:
- ai_response_cache table backing the above.
- users.ai_weekly_summary_enabled (default false) for the parent
  digest opt-in below.

Caching wired into the two highest-value, safely-cacheable call sites:
- reading-game quiz generation: cached by title+author (not user id) --
  every kid who reads the same book now gets the same cached quiz
  instead of a fresh generation. 90-day TTL.
- books/recommend: cached by shelf contents + content restrictions.
  12-hour TTL since a shelf changes often.
Left uncached: pip-chat (conversational, no reuse),
challenges/generate-personal (personalized on live stats, low reuse),
generate-weekend-event (deliberately wants variety week to week --
caching would defeat its own anti-repetition logic).

Writing competition submissions (per the Aug 4 email):
- AI feedback is now feature-flagged off by default
  (ENABLE_AI_STORY_FEEDBACK=true to re-enable). Default path picks
  from 5 hardcoded encouraging messages, zero API calls. Same
  ai_feedback column, so no DB migration needed for this part.

Comment moderation (per the Aug 4 email, plus the safer approach it
asked me to propose before implementing):
- Plain 'skip Gemini after profanity filter passes' would let non-
  profane bullying through uncaught ('nobody likes your story',
  etc.), so this is a 3-stage pipeline instead:
    1. Profanity filter (existing) -- reject immediately, no API call.
    2. New lib/comment-risk-heuristics.ts -- keyword/pattern check for
       non-profane meanness, ALL CAPS, aggressive punctuation. If
       nothing trips it, approve without calling Gemini. This is the
       large majority of comments (genuine, kind feedback).
    3. Gemini -- only for the flagged minority, a real judgment call.
- Also changed the failure behavior: previously, if Gemini was
  unavailable/errored, ANY comment (including ones the old prompt
  would have rejected) fail-opened to approved. Now: only comments
  that never got flagged auto-approve; anything the heuristic flagged
  fails CLOSED (rejected) if Gemini can't be reached, since fail-open
  for a comment already suspected of being unkind is the wrong
  default for a kids' product. Flagging this since it's a behavior
  change beyond just cost-cutting.

Parent weekly digest (per the Aug 4 email):
- AI summaries are now opt-in, default OFF -- every parent gets the
  free template digest unless they've turned on 'Enhanced AI Weekly
  Reading Summary' in Parent Settings (new toggle, optimistic UI,
  reverts on save failure).
- Even when opted in: skipped (falls back to template) when a child
  had no meaningful activity that week, so an idle week doesn't burn
  a call to describe nothing happening.
- Still exactly one Gemini call per parent regardless of child count
  (unchanged -- this was already true). Prompt reworded to be more
  terse (fewer restated instructions) to cut token usage per call.
- Cached per parent+week+data so a duplicate cron execution (retries,
  manual re-runs) reuses the prior result instead of regenerating it.
- Cron schedule, email HTML, and fallback behavior all unchanged.

Not done in this pass -- flagging rather than silently skipping:
- Milestone 4.2 (client-side logic offload) needs a per-feature audit
  I haven't done yet -- didn't want to guess at which client-side
  calculations are safe to move without seeing how they're tested.
- Milestones 4.4/4.5 (daily quotas + rewarded ads) as spec'd don't fit
  cleanly: pip-chat, quizzes, recommendations, and challenge generation
  are all kid-facing, and the landing page explicitly promises kids
  never see ads. Rewarding AI quota with an ad only makes sense on the
  parent/teacher side -- e.g. gating the AI weekly summary itself, or
  a future parent-facing generative feature. Didn't want to build
  ad-gating that contradicts the site's own stated promise without
  checking that's actually what's wanted first.
